narrative:
Aveline doesn't cry this time either, though it costs her visibly more than it did at the gate. She holds onto you a beat longer than the goodbye did, like confirming with her hands what her eyes are already telling her.

@Aveline: You came back. I told myself not to hope for that, and here you are anyway, undoing all my careful not-hoping.

She pulls back, businesslike again almost immediately. @Aveline: Well. Since you're here. The east field's the worst of it. Nessa's been out there every day pretending she knows what she's looking at.

Nessa is exactly where everyone said she'd be, kneeling in the yellowed rows doing something that looks equal parts determined and completely ineffective. She doesn't hear you approach until you're right beside her.

@Nessa: You came back. I knew you would, I told everyone you would, nobody believed me. -then, quieter- Can you actually fix it, or did you just come back to watch it happen with the rest of us?

It's not really a fair question. She asks it anyway, because she's nine years younger and hasn't learned yet which questions aren't fair.

What you learned crouched over a stranger's failing field, days ago and a whole journey removed, turns out to matter here in a way you couldn't have predicted - the same starved, over-planted soil, the same fix, if there's still time for one.

@Nessa: Wait, you already KNEW about this? From somewhere else?

@You: Learned it on the road. Didn't think it'd matter here so soon.

Aldenmoor doesn't come through this season unscathed - no valley does, not this year - but it comes through together, and considerably better than it would have without you, which is a different and more useful thing to have been than merely present for the goodbye.

Aveline finds you the morning you finally, actually leave again, second time being no easier than the first despite everyone's best efforts to pretend otherwise.

@Aveline: Go on, then. Properly this time. You've more than earned the leaving.
